56,1124,9407,856,59,568,3719,3696,10607,419,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,50853,220,17,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,50,6383,7766,279,1290,24413,3108,11,582,614,510,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,5847,11,582,646,8168,700,1124,11520,15940,856,57758,504,2176,11067,315,279,23606,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,8,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,1654,646,55327,844,419,311,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,8,284,220,15,7110,921,1986,23606,6696,601,1378,5048,311,2908,510,16,13,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,1326,17,13,17767,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,284,220,15,59,3593,334,4207,220,16,25,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,568,1019,785,9904,311,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,57758,304,279,9873,17767,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,59,2493,57758,525,17767,87,284,220,15,11,1124,2493,11,220,17,59,2493,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"fefd670c1988b90d8d5c4122c539f1a060ad65667e52c488d500914c630c2ce9"},"seconds":52.37925887107849,"seed":568294847568155975,"stop":"eos","text":"Let's solve the equation step by step:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]\nFirst, recall that \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\nSimplify the right side:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\n\nFactor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\nRearrange the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\)\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\)\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\)**\n\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are:\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\]\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\)**\n\nRewrite \\(\\sin^2 x\\) using the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\):\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\]\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0\\]\n\nLet \\(u = \\cos x\\). The equation becomes:\n\\[-2u^2 + 3u - 1 = 0\\]\nMultiply through by \\(-1\\):\n\\[2u^2 - 3u + 1 = 0\\]\n\nSolve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(u = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2}\\]\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4}\\]\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}\\]\n\\[u = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = \\frac{1}{2}\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n- For \\(\\cos x = 1\\):\n  \\[x = 0, 2\\pi\\]\n\n- For \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\):\n  \\[x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\]\n\nNow, combine all solutions:\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\]\n\nSum these solutions:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is:\n\\[\\boxed{5\\pi}\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Let's solve the equation step by step:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]\nFirst, recall that \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\nSimplify the right side:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\n\nFactor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x\\]\nRearrange the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\)\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\)\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\)**\n\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are:\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\]\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\)**\n\nRewrite \\(\\sin^2 x\\) using the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\):\n\\[2(1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\]\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0\\]\n\nLet \\(u = \\cos x\\). The equation becomes:\n\\[-2u^2 + 3u - 1 = 0\\]\nMultiply through by \\(-1\\):\n\\[2u^2 - 3u + 1 = 0\\]\n\nSolve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(u = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2}\\]\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4}\\]\n\\[u = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}\\]\n\\[u = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = \\frac{1}{2}\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n- For \\(\\cos x = 1\\):\n  \\[x = 0, 2\\pi\\]\n\n- For \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\):\n  \\[x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\]\n\nNow, combine all solutions:\n\\[x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\]\n\nSum these solutions:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is:\n\\[\\boxed{5\\pi}\\]","id":"precalculus:64"}
